<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!--
        The scrim behind the system bars, and the *only* thing that varies between the four copies
        of this file. It exists because `MainActivity` deliberately does not call `enableEdgeToEdge()`.

        That call was the app's edge-to-edge mechanism until Play flagged it: every path in
        androidx.activity 1.13.0 — `EdgeToEdgeApi23`, `Api26`, `Api29` and `Api35` alike — reaches
        `Window.setStatusBarColor` and `setNavigationBarColor`, both deprecated in Android 15, and
        no version of the call avoids them. Verified in the AAR's bytecode rather than assumed.
        `WindowCompat.setDecorFitsSystemWindows(window, false)` replaces the half Compose needs; the
        colours are what is left, and a theme *attribute* is not the deprecated *method*.

        **A scrim rather than transparency below API 29, and that is the load-bearing part.** The
        content scrolls under the status bar, so without one the owner's text slides directly behind
        the clock. From 29 the platform enforces bar contrast itself — `enforceStatusBarContrast`
        defaults false and `enforceNavigationBarContrast` true, which is exactly what an
        edge-to-edge app wants — so the scrim goes transparent there and the system takes over. From
        35 the attribute is ignored altogether and edge-to-edge is the platform's own default.

        The two values are androidx's own `DefaultLightScrim` and `DefaultDarkScrim`, copied so the
        pixels do not change with the mechanism: this is a deprecation fix, not a redesign.

        Four files, because of how Android resolves qualifiers: `night` is a *higher-precedence*
        dimension than `vN`, so `values-night/` would beat `values-v29/` on an API 29+ phone in dark
        mode and hand it a scrim it should not have. `values-night-v29/` is what settles that, and
        it is why this is a colour rather than three items redefined per qualifier — a `<style>`
        cannot merge across qualified files the way one `<color>` reference can.
    -->
    <color name="system_bar_scrim">#E6FFFFFF</color>
</resources>
